  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What a pilot is for</p>
    <h2>Small studies are most useful when they answer design questions</h2>
    <p>The CONSORT extension for randomized pilot and feasibility trials emphasizes that their objectives differ from definitive efficacy trials. A pilot is used to ask whether a future trial can be conducted, whether it should proceed and how its design should change.</p>
    <p>Useful pilot outputs may therefore include recruitment rate, retention, completeness of samples, adherence, acceptability, protocol deviations, missing-data patterns and whether a planned outcome can actually be measured consistently.</p>
    <p>A small apparent difference between groups can be interesting, but a pilot that is not powered for efficacy should not be marketed as though that difference proves effectiveness.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Hydration measurement</p>
    <h2>Build redundancy into the measurement strategy</h2>
    <p>Hydration assessment has no universally perfect field measure. Urine concentration, body-mass change, fluid intake and output, thirst and clinical context can each contribute information while also carrying limitations.</p>
    <p>Our pilot framework therefore favors complementary measures and standardized collection windows. The first question is whether those measurements are feasible and interpretable in the intended setting, not whether a single threshold can label every participant as hydrated or dehydrated.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Progression criteria</p>
  <h2>Decide in advance what would make a pilot advance, change or stop</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Recruitment</h3><p>Can the intended population be identified and recruited at a rate that makes a larger study realistic?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Protocol completion</h3><p>Can participants and staff follow the preparation, timing, sampling and follow-up requirements without excessive burden?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Data quality</h3><p>Are samples, measurements and records sufficiently complete and consistent to support the planned analysis?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Safety & tolerance</h3><p>Are adverse events, gastrointestinal symptoms, stopping reasons and other safety signals captured clearly enough to inform the next stage?</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Research governance</p>
    <h2>Calling a study a “pilot” does not remove ethics or regulatory requirements</h2>
    <p>Requirements depend on what the protocol actually does. If investigators assign an intervention, collect samples beyond routine care or study a natural health product under conditions that trigger Health Canada's clinical-trial requirements, the applicable authorization and Research Ethics Board processes still apply.</p>
    <p>Health Canada's NHP guidance specifically notes that its narrow observational category does not involve investigator-assigned treatment and does not collect samples outside normal medical practice. Additional study-specific blood or urine sampling can change that classification.</p>
    <p>For that reason, public pilot descriptions should be written after protocol classification, not used to define the classification by marketing language.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Naming rule</span>
    <h3>“Pilot” describes scale and purpose, not exemption.</h3>
    <p>Ethics, consent and regulatory requirements follow the actual research activities and product conditions of use.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Pilot reporting</p>
  <h2>What a public study update should eventually contain</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Protocol identity</h3><p>Study title, design, sponsor/investigator roles, setting and registry or approval identifiers where applicable and publicly disclosable.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Predefined feasibility objectives</h3><p>State the questions and progression criteria before presenting findings.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Actual completion status</h3><p>Recruitment, withdrawals, protocol deviations, data completeness and safety/tolerance reporting.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Decision after the pilot</h3><p>Advance unchanged, modify, repeat, narrow the question or stop, with the rationale visible.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

